A||||8||thomas_l||What did I break? (autocrossing)||||5||
Z||000000||thomas_l||12-03-2001||09:02 AM||||After a great day at the autocross I headed home. When merging onto the freeway I noticed that the rear of my 87 GT got <I>really</I> squirrely. <P>It was bad enough that I was listening for signs of a loose wheel or flat tire! Anyway I went a bit further and stopped at a gas station for a quick look over. No flat tires, no loose lug nuts. Shaking the body side to side in the rear didn't really show excessive looseness or any noises. A quick look underneath didn't reveal anything obviously broken or loose. During the day of racing I was turning consistently better times on four excellent runs and felt no signs of anything letting go.<P>I put new wheel bearings in the rear when I put the trans in a few months ago and got it aligned about that time too. The car tracks straight under accel or braking so I'm semi-eliminating control arm bushings and tie rod ends. I have yet to get the car in the air to see if a ball joint is dying.<P>I am hoping someone has had similar rear looseness after spirited driving and might shed some light before I tear it apart.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Nashco||12-03-2001||09:46 AM||||Did you check your motor and transmission mounts?  Our cars have a unique setup, so that if those are badly worn or broken, the rear gets VERY sloppy.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000002||thomas_l||12-03-2001||10:48 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Nashco:<BR><B>Did you check your motor and transmission mounts?  Our cars have a unique setup, so that if those are badly worn or broken, the rear gets VERY sloppy.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Good point - I hadn't thought of that. I didn't notice any "lurching" during accel or decel so I didn't think of motor/trans mounts. I'll be sure to check them when I get it in the air in addition to a visual with the deck open and park brake on  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">. I have OEM mounts on the bottom with a poly dogbone up top.<P>On that note I forgot to mention in the original post - everything's stock (suspension, busings, etc) to the best of my knowledge.||||reg||1
Z||000003||California Kid||12-03-2001||11:04 AM||||Wouldn't hurt to check your cradle bolts while you have it up in the air. Probably best to do a check on everything.<P>Are you sure that the rear wheel bearing were torqued to specification, it's really high foot pound wise.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/californiakidsig_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||SLYGUY||12-03-2001||12:31 PM||||I agree with the tips above but I have one question for you.<P>What is autocrossing?<P>Sly||||reg||1
Z||000005||thomas_l||12-03-2001||01:07 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by SLYGUY:<BR><B>I agree with the tips above but I have one question for you.<P>What is autocrossing?<P>Sly</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>You can read all about it on <A HREF="http://www.scca.org/amateur/solo2/index.html" TARGET=_blank>the SCCA SOLO-II website</A>. You navigate thru traffic cones set up in a parking lot as fast as possible without going off course or hitting cones.||||reg||14
Z||000006||thomas_l||12-03-2001||01:22 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by California Kid:<BR><B>Are you sure that the rear wheel bearing were torqued to specification, it's really high foot pound wise.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Mystery solved and actually you are close. I got a friend to help me do a motor mount test (I power braked and he watched). Mounts were OK. Afterwards we were talking and he kicked the top of a rear wheel and spotted some movement. Looks like I have wiped out the left rear wheel bearing. They were new in April and have less than 2000 miles on them (ok they were <B>really hard</B> miles driven mostly one at a time  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">). I confirmed by seeing the outer CV joint moving with the wheel. Also the sidewalls on that side are scuffed 1/2" lower than the other. Since it was dark after racing I didn't notice that. The bearing must've started letting go on my last run. Since there's no grinding or growling I'll take a chance driving it 6 miles home, easy on the corners  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">.<P>Yes the bearings were torqued to spec when installed. I have a 1/2" drive Snap-on torque wrench that goes up to 250 ft-lb that gets used on the axle nuts and other big fasteners.||||reg||3
Z||000007||Slammed Fiero||12-03-2001||01:23 PM||||Check the rear tie rods , they do have a tendancy to bend. It may not apply in this case , but it can happen in autocrossing. after bending 2 sets I swapped mine to aircraft Aluminum with spherical ends..a lot more controlled feeling , helped to tighten up the rear of the car<P>JM<P>------------------<BR>Jonathan McCreery<BR>86 GT 4spd<BR>Koni's , Coilovers, Big Bars , 17" Trmotorsports Typhoons , 11.25" vented discs, Wilwood 4 Piston Calipers , Pirelli P Zero's, Short Shift, No cat , Cold air induction , Geni Stainless tips, White face gauges , Momo shift , momo pedals, Big Bore TB, 70Hp Shot of NOS Autocrossed , Tracked , Dragged? (soon) abused.||||reg||1
Z||000008||thomas_l||12-03-2001||01:57 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Slammed Fiero:<BR><B>Check the rear tie rods , they do have a tendancy to bend. It may not apply in this case , but it can happen in autocrossing. after bending 2 sets I swapped mine to aircraft Aluminum with spherical ends..a lot more controlled feeling , helped to tighten up the rear of the car<P>JM<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Along that line, I am going to look around and start replacing broken stuff with performance parts instead of smoking warranty claims on OEM stuff. The rear wheel bearings were $85 at Pep Boys and seem just like the FWD units on Cavaliers and Celebrities. They are probably under warranty and exchangeable. I already got NAPA to swap a leaky caliper under warranty. Warranties are great for getting free parts but there were no elves in the box to hang the parts for me  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||10
